\section{Visible and invisible barrier metrics}\label{supp:distance_measuring}
\subsection{Measuring POI Intervening Opportunities}
Intervening opportunities represent alternative destinations or attractions that may divert individuals from their originally intended destination during spatial movement. 
Stouffer's law of intervening opportunities~\cite{stouffer1940intervening} formalizes this concept, stating that ``the number of persons going a given distance is directly proportional to the number of opportunities at that distance and inversely proportional to the number of intervening opportunities.''
This principle captures the fundamental trade-off between destination attractiveness and the availability of alternative opportunities along the travel path.

In our analysis, we quantify POI intervening opportunity as the total count of POIs located within the convex hull formed by a pair of CBGs inclusively, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{supp_fig:poi_inter}.
This geometric approach estimates the potential destinations that could reasonably divert movement between the origin and destination CBG, as well as those in the potential travel corridor.
To prevent multicollinearity problems with ``\# Park Crossed'' in the model given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:logistic}, POIs wtih category of ``City / Outdoors'' are excluded from the POI Intervening Opportunity.

