\section{Cross Barrier Ratio}\label{supp:cbr_definition}
Fig.~\ref{fig:4}a summarizes the workflow used to compute the cross-barrier ratio (CBR). Because the origin–destination matrix is highly sparse—only \(3\%\) of CBG pairs register at least one trip in the 2019 baseline—most candidate barriers identified by large positive residuals (top \(5\%\); see Methods) correspond to dyads with zero observed flow.  
To obtain a sample large enough for statistically reliable estimates of the CBR, we therefore  
(i) restrict attention to CBG pairs separated by less than 20 km that exhibit non-zero flow and  
(ii) recompute residuals via $\hat{d}_{ij}^{(e)} \sim \beta \log(d_{ij}^{(p)}) + \epsilon_{ij}$.  
Pairs whose residual now falls in the upper quartile (top \(25\%\)) of this restricted set are labelled soft barriers.  
For every user \(i\) we follow their full trajectories and evaluate the individual CBR, \(r_i\).  
Overall, \(56.5\%\) of users never cross a soft barrier (\(r_i = 0\)) in 2019 study period.  
The CBSA-level distribution of CBR values is displayed in Fig.~\ref{supp_fig:CBR_dist}.

