\begin{document}
\title{Fixed Points of the Josephus Function via Fractional Base Expansions}
\author{Yunier Bello-Cruz\footnote{Department of Mathematical Sciences, Northern Illinois Unversity, USA (yunierbello@niu.edu)} \qquad Roy Quintero-Contreras\footnote{Department of Mathematical Sciences, Northern Illinois Unversity, USA (rquinterocontreras@niu.edu)} }
\date{} 

\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we investigated some interesting properties of the fixed points of the Josephus function $J_3$. First, we establish a connection between this sequence and the Chinese Remainder Theorem. Next, we observed a clear numerical pattern in the fixed points sequence when the terms are written in base $3/2$ using modular arithmetic, which allows us to develop  a recursive procedure to determine the digits of their base $3/2$ expansions.\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

This work discusses some interesting properties of the Josephus function $J_3$, which arises from the classical Josephus problem, a well-known mathematical riddle originating from a narrative by the Jewish-Roman historian Flavius Josephus (see \cite [Book 3, Chapter 8, Part 7]{Fla}). The problem is described as follows: Given a group of $n$ people seated at a round table, numbered consecutively clockwise order from $1$ to $n$, begin counting from position $1$. Skip two people and eliminate the third. Continue this process with the remaining participants, always starting with the next person, counting two, and removing the third, proceeding clockwise until only one person remains. This final individual is referred to as the survivor.

A natural question that arises is: Where should a person initially sit to avoid being eliminated? We denote the survivor's position by $J_3(n)$. Determining this position directly for large $n$ can be computationally expensive.  To address this, a particular sequence of fixed points $\{n_p^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell\in\mathbb{N}}$ of  $J_3$ was introduced in \cite{Bel2, Bel}, where each term satisfies the condition $n_p^{(\ell)}=J_3(n_p^{(\ell)})$. These fixed points not only help characterize the behavior of the function $J_3$, but also provide insight into its recursive and arithmetic structure.  

Next, we present the main section of this paper, which is organized into three subsections. The first connects the search for fixed points of $J_3$ with the well-known Chinese Remainder Theorem, allowing us to represent each fixed point as a solution to a system of congruences with coprime moduli. The second introduces a kind of ``modular'' numbering system with respect to the base $3/2$, following the approach presented in \cite{Aki, Ros}, and investigates its relevance in the context of fixed points. Finally, the third subsection uses such a numbering system to recursively determine the digits of the expansion of fixed points of $J_3$.  


\section{Main Results} 

\subsection{Fixed points of $J_3$ and the Chinese Remainder Theorem}

We begin by recalling the recursive formula for the fixed point sequence \( \{n_p^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell \in \mathbb{N}} \) of the function \( J_3 \), as stated in~\cite[Theorem 2]{Bel}. Starting at \( n_p^{(1)} = 1 \), we compute
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align} \label{fixpointseq}
n_{p}^{(\ell+1)} &= \frac{3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}(3n_{p}^{(\ell)} + 2) - 2^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}}{2^{\overline{m}_{\ell} + 1}}, \\
\label{m-l-formula}
\overline{m}_{\ell} &= \max \left\{ m \in \mathbb{Z}_+ \mid 2^m \text{ divides } 3n_p^{(\ell)} + 2 \right\}.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
for every \( \ell \in \mathbb{N} \).
 From equation~\eqref{fixpointseq}, we can derive, after a few algebraic manipulations, the equivalent identity
\begin{equation}\label{eq-fixpointseq}
2^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}(2n_{p}^{(\ell+1)}+1)=3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}(3n_{p}^{(\ell)} + 2),
\end{equation}
which tells us that $n_{p}^{(\ell+1)}$ and $n_{p}^{(\ell)}$ are each a solution to the following system of linear congruences:
\begin{subequations}\label{formula 4}
\begin{empheq}[left=\empheqlbrace]{align}
2x_1 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}} \label{formula 4-A} \\
3x_2 + 2 &\equiv 0 \pmod{2^{\overline{m}_{\ell}}}. \label{formula 4-B}
\end{empheq}
\end{subequations}
Even more, if we apply the same argument to $n_{p}^{(\ell+2)}$ and $n_{p}^{(\ell+1)}$, we have that they are each a solution to:
\begin{subequations}\label{formula 4'}
\begin{empheq}[left=\empheqlbrace]{align}
2x_1 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{3^{\overline{m}_{\ell+1}}} \label{formula 4-C} \\
3x_2 + 2 &\equiv 0 \pmod{2^{\overline{m}_{\ell+1}}}. \label{formula 4-D}
\end{empheq}
\end{subequations}
Furthermore, since $n_{p}^{(\ell+1)}$ satisfies both \eqref{formula 4-A} and \eqref{formula 4-D}, it must also satisfies the following system of congruences:
\[
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
2x_1 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{3^p} \\
3x_2 + 2 &\equiv 0 \pmod{2^q},
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]
where $p:=\overline{m}_{\ell}$ and $q:=\overline{m}_{\ell +1}$. Note that when $pq=0$, our argument does make no sense, absolutely. From this point on, we can assume that $p, q \geq 1$. Independently, by induction on $p$ and $q$, we can prove that 
\[
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
2x_1 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{3^p} 
&&\Longleftrightarrow\quad x_1 \equiv a_1 := \frac{1}{2}(3^p - 1) \pmod{3^p} \\[0.36cm]
3x_2 + 2 &\equiv 0 \pmod{2^q} 
&&\Longleftrightarrow\quad x_2 \equiv a_2 := 
\begin{cases} 
\dfrac{2}{3}(2^q - 1) \pmod{2^q}, & \text{if } q \equiv 0 \pmod{2} \\[0.36cm]
\dfrac{2}{3}(2^{q-1} - 1) \pmod{2^q}, & \text{if } q \equiv 1 \pmod{2}.
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]
Therefore, the fixed point $n_{p}^{(\ell+1)}$ satisfies the following system of linear congruences: 
\begin{equation}\label{formula 6}
\begin{cases}
\ x_1 \equiv a_1 \pmod{3^{p}}  & \\
\ x_2 \equiv a_2 \pmod{2^{q}}. & 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Since $3^p$ and $2^q$ are coprime positive integers, i.e., $\gcd(3^p, 2^q)=1$, we can apply the classical form of the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) (see \cite [p. 294]{Rosen}). This guarantees a unique solution $z$ to \eqref{formula 6} modulo $3^p \times 2^q$, given by $z=a_12^qy+a_23^px,$
where $x$ and $y$ are a pair of integers satisfying Bézout's identity (see \cite[p. 285]{Rosen}): 
\begin{equation}\label{Bezout-Id}3^px+2^qy=\gcd(3^p, 2^q)=1,\end{equation} where $\gcd$ is the greatest common divisor.
In this way, we uncover an interesting connection between the sequence  $\{n_p^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell\in \mathbb{N}}$ and CRT. 
Moreover, since the explicit expression for $z$ depends on a solution to Bézout's identity, it is natural to examine how $1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $3^p$ and $2^q$.
