Table \ref{table 1} provides Bézout's linear combinations for all  $p\in\mathbb{N}$ and for $q = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5$ in a compact form.
We invite the interested reader to extend Table \ref{table 1} to other values of $q$. 
\begin{remark}
For illustrating two instances in Table \ref{table 1} below, consider  the values \( n_{p}^{(17)} = 3 986 218 \) and \( n_{p}^{(18)} = 102 162 424 \) given in \cite[Table 1]{Bel2} or Table \ref{table 2} below, where $p=7$ (i.e.,  $\overline{m}_{17}$) and $q=1$ (i.e., $\overline{m}_{18}$), respectively. According to \eqref{formula 6} and Table \ref{table 1}, \( a_1 = 1093 \), \( a_2 = 0 \), and $x=1$ and $y=-1093$. Therefore, $$z= 1093\times 2^1 \times (-1093) + 0 \times 3^7 \times 1=-2389298 \equiv 3280 \pmod{4374}.$$ Thus, $n_{p}^{(18)}=102162424$ must be equivalent to 3280 modulo 4374, which is indeed true since $(102162424 - 3280)/4374=23356$.

Another illustrative example which can be easily derived is when \( n_{p}^{(13)} = 46084 \) and \( n_{p}^{(14)} = 103690 \); see Table \ref{table 2} below. In this case, \( p = 1 \) (i.e., \( \overline{m}_{13} = 1 \)) and \( q = 5 \) (i.e., \( \overline{m}_{14} = 5 \)). Here, \( a_1 = 1 \), \( a_2 = 10 \) in \eqref{formula 6}, and Bézout identity \eqref{Bezout-Id} to get \( 3^1 \cdot 11 + 2^5 \cdot (-1) = 33 - 32 = 1 \) implies \( x = 11 \), \( y = -1 \). So,
\[
z = 1 \times 2^5 \times (-1) + 10 \times 3^1 \times 11 = 298 \equiv 10 \pmod{96}.
\]
Hence, \( n_{p}^{(14)}= 103690 \) must be congruent to $10$ modulo $96$. Indeed, 
$(103690 - 10)/96 = 1080$.

\end{remark}

\subsection{A modular expansion of any natural number in base $3/2$}
We begin this subsection by noting that, if we slightly manipulate the right-hand side of~\eqref{fixpointseq} algebraically, we obtain the following expression:
\begin{equation}\label{formula 7}
n_{p}^{(\ell+1)} = \left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{\overline{m}_{\ell}+1} n_{p}^{(\ell)} + \left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{\overline{m}_{\ell}} - \frac{1}{2},
\end{equation}
which provides motivation for studying representations of natural numbers in base $3/2$. Motivated by \eqref{formula 7} and the basic conceptual framework presented in the introduction to \cite [Section 3]{Ros}, we propose a change from base $-3/2$ to base $3/2$, which will allow us to characterize the fixed points of $J_3$ in a much simpler way. Here, we do not refer to the relatively well-known `fractional' numerical system with respect to base $3/2$ with digits $0$ or $1$. To recall how such a system works, let us choose the natural number $4$ and represent it in base $3/2$. Clearly, a good default approximation of this number is $1000.01001001$ because 
$$\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^3+\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{-2}+\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{-5}+\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)^{-8}=3.99015012955.$$
Instead of relying on that system, we aim to represent any positive integer $N$ in the form:
\begin{equation}\label{expansion}
 N= \frac{1}{2}\left [d_k \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k}+ d_{k-1} \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k-1} + \cdots + d_1 \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^1+d_0\right],   
\end{equation}
where $d_i \in \mathcal{D}=\{0, 1, 2\}$ for some $k\in \mathbb{Z}_+$. If $k\geq 1$, we always assume that $d_k \neq 0$. When $N$ has the expansion of the form \eqref{expansion} we denote:
$N=(d_k\cdots d_0)_{3/2}.$

 To compute an expansion of the form \eqref{expansion}, we use the following four-step inductive algorithm, presented in \cite[p. 61]{Aki}: 

 \medskip

\noindent    \underline{\bf Step 1}: Set $N_0 = N$ and write $2N_0=3N_1 +d_0$ with $d_0 \in \mathcal{D}$ and $N_1 \in \mathbb{Z}_+$. Note that $d_0$ is the unique digit in $\mathcal{D}$ satisfying the congruence $d_0 = {\rm mod}(2N_0, 3)$ and thus, $N_1$ is also uniquely determined. 

\medskip

 \noindent     \underline{\bf Step 2}: Define the non-negative integers $N_1, N_2, \ldots$ recursively by the equations $2N_i=3N_{i+1} +d_i,$
    with $d_i \in \mathcal{D}$, uniquely defined as $d_i ={\rm mod}(2N_i, 3)$.

    \medskip

\noindent      \underline{\bf Step 3}: We now prove by induction on $i$ that 
    \begin{equation}\label{formula 9}
        N=\left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{i+1} N_{i+1}+\frac{1}{2}\left [d_i \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{i}+ \cdots + d_1 \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)+d_0\right].
    \end{equation}
We verify the base case $i=0$: from {\bf Step 1}, $$N=N_0=\frac{3}{2}N_1+\frac{1}{2}d_0,$$ which confirms that \eqref{formula 9} holds for $i=0$.
Now assume that \eqref{formula 9} holds for $i=k\geq 0$.
From {\bf Step 2}, we have 
$$2N_{k+1}=3N_{k+2} +d_{k+1}.$$ Substituting this into the inductive hypothesis gives us:
\begin{align*}
    N&=\left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k+1} N_{k+1}+\frac{1}{2}\left [d_k \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k}+ \cdots + d_1 \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)+d_0\right] \\
     &=\left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k+1}\left(\frac{3}{2}N_{k+2}+\frac{1}{2}d_{k+1} \right) +\frac{1}{2}\left [d_k \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k}+ \cdots + d_1 \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)+d_0\right] \\    
     &=\left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k+2} N_{k+2}+\frac{1}{2}\left [d_{k+1} \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)^{k+1}+ \cdots + d_1 \left(\frac{3}{2} \right)+d_0\right],
\end{align*}
which proves that \eqref{formula 9} holds for $i=k+1$. Thus, by induction, equation \eqref{formula 9} holds for all $i\geq 0$.

    \medskip

\noindent\underline{\bf Step 4}: We show that $N_{\hat i} = 0$ for some $\hat i$, ensuring that the algorithm terminates and yields a representation of the form \eqref{expansion}. 

From the recurrence relation $$N_{i+1} = \frac{2}{3}N_i - \frac{1}{3}d_i \le \frac{2}{3}N_i,$$ the sequence $\{N_i\}$ is strictly decreasing whenever $N_i \geq 3$. This guarantees that, after finitely many steps, we will reach a value $N_{i_0} \in \{1, 2\}$ for some $i_0 \geq 1$. We consider the two possible cases: (i) If $N_{i_0} = 2$ then $N_{i_0+1} = 1$ and $N_{i_0+2} = 0$, so the expansion terminates at $\hat i=i_0+2$. (ii) If $N_{i_0} = 1$ then $N_{i_0+1} = 0$, hence, $\hat i=i_0+1$.

Therefore, for any initial value $N = N_0 \in \mathbb{N}$, the sequence $\{N_i\}$ eventually reaches zero, which confirms that $N$ has a finite expansion of the form \eqref{expansion}.
Since each digit $d_i$ is uniquely determined by the congruence $d_i \equiv 2N_i \pmod{3}$ and must lie in $\mathcal{D} = \{0,1,2\}$, it follows that the expansion is unique.

