\subsection{Characterization of  $\{n_p^{(\ell)}\}_{\ell\in \mathbb{N}}$ through the base $3/2$ number system}

Observing the modular base $3/2$ expansions of the first twenty fixed points of $J_3$ (see Table \ref{table 2}), we are led to believe that there is a natural connection between these expansions and the divisibility condition that defines the sequence $\{\overline{m}_{\ell}\}_{\ell\in \mathbb{N}}$ (see equation \eqref{m-l-formula}). In particular, we have underlined some of the last digits of the expansions as shown in Table \ref{table 2}.  We now state and prove the main result of this work, which confirms and formalizes these observations.

\begin{theorem} 
Assume that the base $3/2$ expansion of the fixed point $n_p^{(\ell)}$ of the Josephus function $J_3$ is given by
$
n_p^{(\ell)} = (\hat{d_k} \cdots \hat{d_{0}})_{3/2}.
$
Then, the following statements hold:
    \item [ {\bf (i)}] If $\overline{m}_{\ell}=0$, then $n_p^{(\ell+1)}=(\hat{d_k} \cdots \hat{d_{0}} \, 1)_{3/2}$.
    \item [ {\bf (ii)}]  If $\overline{m}_{\ell}= 1$, then $n_p^{(\ell+1)}=(\hat{d_k} \cdots \hat{d_{0}} \, 0 \, 2)_{3/2}$.
    \item [ {\bf (iii)}]  If $\overline{m}_{\ell}\geq 2$, then $n_p^{(\ell+1)}=(\hat{d_k} \cdots \hat{d_{0}} \, 0 \! \underbrace{1 \cdots 1}_{\text{$(\overline{m}_{\ell}-1)$ ones}} \! 2)_{3/2}$.
    \item [ {\bf (iv)}]  The total number of digits in the base $3/2$ expansion of the fixed point $n_p^{(\ell+1)}$ is always equal to the number of digits in the base $3/2$ expansion of its predecessor plus $\overline{m}_{\ell} + 1$.  
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
To prove {\bf (i)} let $\overline{m}_{\ell}=0$. Then, by \eqref{eq-fixpointseq}, $2n_p^{(\ell+1)}=3n_p^{(\ell)}+1$. So, $d_0= 1$, and in the induction formula \eqref{formula 9}, with $N=n_p^{(\ell+1)}$ and $N_1=n_p^{(\ell)}$ proving this case.

To prove {\bf (ii)} and {\bf (iii)},  assume that $\overline{m}_{\ell}\geq 1$. We set $N_0=n_p^{(\ell+1)}.$ For simplicity, we define $$A_i=\dfrac{3n_p^{(\ell)}+2}{2^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-i}}$$ for $i=0, 1, \ldots, \overline{m}_{\ell}$. Note that the definition of $\overline{m}_\ell$ in \eqref{m-l-formula} implies that $A_i\in \mathbb{N}$ for all $i=0, 1, \ldots, \overline{m}_{\ell}$.  Then, by \eqref{eq-fixpointseq}, $$2N_0=3(3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}A_0)-1=3(3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}A_0-1)+2.$$ So, $d_0={\rm mod}(2N_{0}, 3)=2$ and $N_1=3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}A_0-1$ by observing the recursion \eqref{formula 9}. If $\overline{m}_{\ell}=1$, $2N_{1}=2A_{0}-2=A_{1}-2=3n_p^{(\ell)}$. Therefore, $d_{1}={\rm mod}(2N_{1},3)=0$. Hence, $d_{1}=0$ and the digits 2 and 0 are added to the right of the base $3/2$ expansion of $N_{2}=n_p^{(\ell)}$ to represent $n_p^{(\ell+1)}$, establishing item {\bf (ii)}.

 If  $\overline{m}_{\ell}\ge 2$, we can repeat the algorithm $\overline{m}_{\ell}-1$ times starting at:

\underline{Step 1}: Here, we have   $$2N_1=3(3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-2}A_1)-2=3(3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-2}A_1-1)+1.$$ Then, $d_1={\rm mod}(2N_1,3)=1$ and $N_2=3^{\overline{m}_{\ell}-2}A_1-1$. Until, 
\underline{Step $\overline{m}_{\ell}-1$}: where we get,     $$2N_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}=3A_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}-2=3(A_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}-1)+1.$$ Hence, $d_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}={\rm mod}(2N_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1},3)=1$ and $N_{\overline{m}_{\ell}}=A_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}-1$.

     Finally, by applying the algorithm over again, we get $$2N_{\overline{m}_{\ell}}=2A_{\overline{m}_{\ell}-1}-2=A_{\overline{m}_{\ell}}-2=3n_p^{(\ell)}.$$ So, $d_{\overline{m}_{\ell}}=0$ and $N_{\overline{m}_{\ell}+1}=n_p^{(\ell)}$ proving item {\bf (iii)}.

The item {\bf (iv)} follows directly from (i), (ii), and (iii) , as each case increases the digit count of the base $3/2$ expansion by exactly $\overline{m}_{\ell}$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
The result presented in the previous theorem can be seen as a generalization of the closed-form expression for the fixed points of the Josephus function \( J_2 \), as discussed in \cite{Knuth, Gra}. In this case, the fixed points are given by $n_p^{(\ell)} = 2^\ell - 1$,
and \( \overline{m}_\ell = 0 \) for all \( \ell \in \mathbb{N} \), since all high extremal points are fixed points for $J_2$. Therefore, their expansions in the well-known binary base \( 2 \) exhibit a particularly simple recursive pattern. Specifically, if
$n_p^{(\ell)} = (\hat{b}_k \cdots \hat{b}_0)_{2}$
is the base-2 expansion of the \( \ell \)-th fixed point, i.e., $\hat{b}_i\in \{0,1\}$ for all $i$, then the next fixed point has the expansion
$n_p^{(\ell+1)} = (\hat{b}_k \cdots \hat{b}_0\, 1)_{2}$,
that is, the digit \( 1 \) is appended to the right of the previous expansion. Consequently, the number of digits in base-2 for \( n_p^{(\ell)} \) is exactly \( \ell \), and each fixed point is constructed by adding one digit to the expansion of its predecessor. Moreover, \( \hat{b}_i = 1 \) for all \( i\) as shown in the following table:
