Moreover, in \cite{Knuth, Gra}, the binary expansion of $n$ can be used to compute the solution of the Josephus problem $J_2(n)$ by shifting its digits. Indeed, if $n=(b_k \cdots b_0)_{2}$ and $j:=\max\left\{j\in \{k-1,\ldots,0\right\} \mid b_j\neq 0\}$, then $J_2(n)=(b_{j} \cdots b_0 b_k)_{2}$. 

It remains an open question whether: (i) the base $3/2$ expansions of $n$ and $n_p^{(\ell+1) }$, together with the information of $\overline{m}_\ell$, can be used to solve the Josephus problem, $J_3(n)$. Equations (9) and (10) in \cite{Bel} may provide a method to determine the base $3/2$ expansion of $J_3(n)$; (ii) the fixed points of the Josephus function \( J_4 \) admit a recursive digit formula when expressed in base-$4/3$ number system. Exploring such a representation could reveal a structural pattern, analogous to those observed in the base $3/2$ expansions of the fixed points of \( J_3 \), which generalize the case of \( J_2 \) in base \( 2 \).
\end{remark}


\section{Concluding Remarks}

In this paper, we studied the sequence of fixed points of the Josephus function \( J_3 \) and showed how its structure can be better understood through number theory and a non-integer base representation. First, we used the Chinese Remainder Theorem to describe each fixed point as the solution of a system of two linear congruences, exploiting the fact that the moduli involved are coprime. Then, we explored a numerical pattern that appears when the fixed points are expressed in base $3/2$ and provided a recursive formula to compute the digits of this expansion. 

This connection offers a new and simple way to describe the fixed points of \( J_3 \), and opens the door to further analysis using tools from fractional number systems. Overall, the use of base $3/2$ provides a helpful perspective for understanding the behavior of the fixed points and suggests interesting directions for future research. 

One such direction is to explore whether the fixed points of \( J_4 \) admit a recursive formula in base-$4/3$, potentially revealing patterns similar to those found in \( J_3 \) and \( J_2 \). Another open and promising direction is determining if the Josephus function itself (i.e., determining \( J_3(n) \)) can be carried out using base $3/2$ representations. \\


