\subsection{Environment Design}

\para{Procedural Map Generation.}
A distinctive feature of \crewwildfire is its scalable generative map capability. Each scenario is procedurally generated using Perlin noise to produce continuous terrain features (i.e., elevation, wind, moisture) and discrete land types (i.e., forest, brush, rock, water). Human settlements are also randomly placed controlled by seeds. These variables influence fire spread, visibility, and mission-critical decisions, ensuring each run presents unique and uncertain challenges.

\para{Wildfire Simulation.}
We simulate fire dynamics using an advanced cellular automata model that incorporates slope, wind, vegetation type, moisture, and terrain features. This model creates realistic and unpredictable fire spread, forcing agents to adapt and exploit environmental features such as firebreaks. Specifically, we model the local fire spread probability \( p_{\text{spread}} \) from a source cell to a neighboring target cell as:

\[\label{cellular automata}
p_{\text{spread}} = \theta_{\text{slope}} \cdot \frac{\text{moisture}}{\text{moisture\_constant}} \cdot \left( 
\frac{\vec{w}}{\|\vec{w}\|} \cdot \frac{\Delta \vec{x}}{\|\Delta \vec{x}\|} + 1 \right)
\]

where \( \vec{w} \) denotes the wind vector and \( \Delta \vec{x} \) is the direction vector pointing from the source cell to the target cell. The term \( \theta_{\text{slope}} \) scales the influence based on terrain steepness, while the moisture ratio attenuates the likelihood of ignition in wetter regions. The dot product modulates the alignment of wind and spread direction, increasing fire spread probability when aligned and decreasing it when opposing. This computed value \( p_{\text{spread}} \) is then compared against a threshold to determine whether the target cell ignites in the next simulation step.



