Figure \ref{fig:ecc_zmax} shows the distribution of our stars in the $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$-- eccentricity plane. For the RPM sample, as discussed in \citetalias{Queiroz2021}, most of the stars have high eccentricity and low $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$\, i.e. ecc > 0.7 and $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$ < 2 kpc (cells I, F). However, we note here that there are two differences compared to the \citetalias{Queiroz2021} analysis -- first: as described in Sec. \ref{fig:data intro} the adopted Galactic potential although very similar is slightly different and second: we select only stars confined within 5 kpc, this excludes a large fraction of disc and halo stars (see also Appendix \ref{sec:nonConfined}). For the RVS sample most of the stars have high eccentricity and low $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$ with ecc > 0.5 and 1 < $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$ < 3 kpc (cells C, E, F). In the middle and right panels we show our stars in the plane color coded by their individual \feh\, and \alphafe\, respectively. A general trend is visible, for both \feh\, and \alphafe, showing metal-rich and low-\alphafe\, closer to the Galactic plane while higher $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$ populated by metal-poor and high-\alphafe\, stars, but interestingly the well populated cells also show a clear mixture of high and low-\alphafe\, and metal-poor and metal-rich stars in these cells. In \citetalias{Queiroz2021} the authors showed that the \alphafe\, bimodality is strongly present in all their well populated cells (i.e. with $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$ < 2 kpc and ecc > 0.3). These cells also had a large fraction of stars with high probability to support the bar. Next, we will analyse the MDFs and the velocities for each cell to better understand the constituent populations.

Figure \ref{fig:mdf_ecc_zmax} shows the MDFs for the different cells across the $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$--eccentricity plane. The orange histogram represents the full Bulge sample while individual RVS (black) and RPM (green) are also shown. Our analysis will be based on the full sample, but here it is important to note the contribution from the RVS sample especially to the cells sparsely populated by the RPM (i.e. cells A, B, C, D and E). The authors in \citetalias{Queiroz2021}, find hints of a non-negligible contribution from a spheroid--like component exhibiting a distinct shape in the \alphafe\,vs \feh\,plane, however refrain from any strong conclusions due to low statistics in these cells -- with the RVS sample we have significantly increased the statistics for these cells.

