In Figure \ref{fig:bulge_disc}, we present the chemical, kinematic, and spatial properties of the three Galactic bulge components. For each component, a row of panels (from left to right) shows: the \alphafe\ distribution and the MDF, both decomposed using GMM; the distribution of the three Galactocentric velocity components; the spatial extent derived from the orbital parameters $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$, $R_{\mathrm{apo}}$, and $R_{\mathrm{guide}}$; and the orbital density projections in face-on (XY) and edge-on (XZ) views.

\textbf{Spheroidal bulge:} The spheroidal bulge component primarily constitutes of moderately metal-poor stars with high-\alphafe\ abundances. About 80\% of the stars have high-\alphafe\ ($\mu\approx0.25$) and the rest have low-\alphafe\ ($\mu\approx0.0$) abundances. The MDF can be fitted by three gaussian components - the main component with 60\% weight shows a peak at $\sim-0.60$ dex. The other two MDF components each with $\approx20\%$ weight have the GMM means at $-1.20$ dex and $\approx0.20$ dex respectively\footnote{(which are similar to the halo and the SMR component of the bar) -- this hints at a possible contamination from the halo and the bar.}. The stars show very high radial and vertical velocity dispersion with $\sigma_{V_{R}} \approx \sigma_{V_{R}}\approx100$\kms\ which support our classification of this group as the pressure-supported spheroidal bulge (slighly triaxial -- see further discussion) component and are comparable to literature values (e.g. \citealt{Arentsen2024} and references therein). The azimuthal velocity shows narrow distribution centered at 0 \kms\ with a smaller dispersion $\approx60$\kms\ because of the steps we adopted for the classification which include a cut in the apocenter radius which removes disc and halo contaminants, removal of the bar and the inner-thick disc stars and also a limit of the net angular momentum parameter, favoring the exclusion of stars with high positive/negative rotational velocities from the parent population. The distribution of orbital parameter $Z_{\mathrm{max}}$, $R_{\mathrm{apo}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{guide}}$ and the orbital density projections reveal a slightly-oblate spheroidal shape which extends $\approx3$ kpc from the galactic plane and the average radii (i.e. $R_{\mathrm{guide}}$) also show similar distribution to $\approx3$ kpc. We measure overall vertical and radial metallicity gradients of $\nabla[Fe/H]/\nabla{Z_{\mathrm{max}}} = -0.35\pm0.02$ dex/kpc and $\nabla[Fe/H]/\nabla{R_{\mathrm{apo}}} = -0.19\pm0.01$ dex/kpc\footnote{These gradient measurements however require larger samples to be robust, which will be the topic of a forthcoming paper.\label{fn:grad}}. The $R_{\mathrm{apo}}$ show an extension in the radial direction, typical of triaxial component (see discussion in next Sections). 

\textbf{Inner-thick disc:} The inner-thick disc, shown in the middle row of Fig. \ref{fig:bulge_disc}, also contains primarily metal-poor, high-\alphafe\ stars, but with notable differences in its chemical makeup. The \alphafe\ distribution is best fit by three components, including a distinct intermediate-\alphafe\ group centered at $\sim$0.15 dex, in addition to peaks at $\sim$0.0 and $\sim$0.25 dex, similar to the spheroidal bulge. The disc MDF shows a larger number of stars with sub-solar metallicity (i.e. $-0.5<\feh<0.0$) and the GMM decomposition yields peaks at [Fe/H] $\sim -1.0$, $\sim -0.45$, and $\sim +0.10$ dex, with the intermediate component ($\sim -0.45$ dex) dominating ($\sim60\%$ weight) -- this main peak is at lower value than that of the spheroidal bulge. The most metal-poor MDF component centered at $\approx-1.0$ dex is at slightly higher values and of lower weight compared to the spheroidal bulge. The appearance of the sub-solar \feh\ stars with intermediate \alphafe\ abundance hints at the slowdown of the early high/bursty star-formation which had contributed to the sustained SNe II production of alpha elements as the disc saw increased \feh\ enrichment - this gives rise to the steeper decrease of \alphafe\ with \feh. The disc has a mean $V_{\phi}$ of 133 \kms\ and velocity dispersions $\sigma_{V_R}=66$, $\sigma_{V_{\phi}}=39$ and $\sigma_{V_{Z}}=59$\,\kms\ which are significantly lower than the spheroidal bulge. Compared to the bar (see the next paragraph), the disc has $\overline{V_{\phi}}$ higher by $\approx25$\kms\ and $\sigma_{V_{\phi}}$ lower by $\approx20$\kms\ while the vertical and the radial dispersions are similar. Most disc stars lie within $Z_{\mathrm{max}} < 2$\ kpc, with a concentration in the range $1 \lesssim Z_{\mathrm{max}} \lesssim 2$\ kpc, and extend radially beyond the bar, with $R_{\mathrm{apo}} > 4$\ kpc. A small group of stars are confined to $Z_{\mathrm{max}}<1$\ kpc and could possible be a contamination form the bar or could belong to the thin disc. We measure an overall vertical metallicity gradient of $\nabla [Fe/H]/\nabla Z_{\mathrm{max}} = -0.35\pm0.01$ dex/kpc\footref{fn:grad}. The orbital projections confirm a thick disc-like morphology. The kinematic properties and the high eccentricities $\sim0.7$ for the orbits (see Fig. \ref{fig:3components}) inform us that the inner-thick disc is kinematically hotter than the local thick disc.

\textbf{Bar:} The galactic bar, shown in the last row of Fig. \ref{fig:bulge_disc}, has a starkly different chemical makeup. The \alphafe\ distribution is best fit by three components with two equally weighted ($\approx40\%$ ) low and high-\alphafe\ components centered at 0.0 dex at 0.23 dex respectively. There is an intermediate component, similar to the inner-thick disc, centered at 0.10 dex with 15\% weight. In contrast to the bulge and the disc, the bar has a large fraction of high metallicity ($\feh>0.0$) stars and it's MDF is best described by a 4-component GMM with peaks approximately at $+0.40$, $0.0$, $-0.40$ and, $-0.70$\ dex, contributing roughly 30\%, 25\%, 30\%, and 15\% of the total weight, respectively. The two GMM peaks at $-0.40$ and, $-0.70$\ dex are strikingly close to the MDF peaks for the inner-thick disc and the spheroidal bulge populations - this hints at the possible contributions of these stellar populations to the galactic bar. The most-metal-poor stars with $\feh\lesssim -1.0$\ dex contribute least to the bar, more to the inner thick disc, and most significantly to the spheroidal bulge component. The bar shows a wide spread in azimuthal velocity with a mean of about 110\kms\ which is about 25\kms\ slower than the disc. However, the different orbit family members cover different radii along the bar and provide separate contributions to the overall bar morphology and for the different orbit family members we find a varying trend - the $\overline{V_{\phi}}$ ($\sigma_{V_{\phi}}$) increases (decreases) with increasing $f_{z}/f_{z}$ ratio (see Table \ref{tab:orb_kinematics}). The bar, elongated in face-on view and showing the boxy/peanut exterior with central X morphology, appears as the most compact among the three components with it's stars confined within 4 kpc radially and $\approx$1.5 kpc from the galactic plane.


