\subsection{\label{sec4b}Different Nambu Surfaces}
As discussed in the section \ref{sec2} that doublet $h= \left(H_{1},H_{2}\right)$ is not unique and can have many possible sets, transformed by a Jacobian matrix with its determinant equals to one. This condition makes a restricted class of transformation. It falls under SL(2,R) group. Therefore we can write

\begin{equation}
	h'=Ah 	\Rightarrow \mqty(H_{1}'\\ H_{2}')
	=\mqty(\alpha & \beta \\ \gamma & \delta) \mqty(H_{1} \\ H_{2})
\end{equation}

The transformation matrix $A$ has the general form of \quad A=$\mqty(\alpha & \beta \\ \gamma & \delta)$ , $\forall  \alpha, \beta,\gamma,\delta \in R $ : $(\alpha\delta-\beta\gamma) =1 $ . By the various choices of $\alpha, \beta,\gamma,\delta$ with the above restriction, we can construct different sets of doublets. The condition on the Jacobian clearly shows that the two Hamiltonians are interchangeable under the choice of $ A=\mqty(0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0)$. This means we can represent the two constant energy surfaces by a single surface equation involving the transformation matrix elements. Thus for the present four-wing system, the equations(\ref{hh}) can be casted into the form
\begin{equation}\label{S1}
 S(x, y, z;\alpha, \beta )=\frac{1}{2}\alpha x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\beta fy^ {2}+ \frac{1}{2}\left(\beta-\alpha\frac{c}{f}\right) z ^{2}- \beta  bz 
\end{equation}
This corresponds to a quadratic surface $H_{1}$, while replacing $\alpha$ and $\beta$  with $\gamma$ and $\delta$ respectively gives $H_{2}$ surface. It is clear that with $\alpha = 1$ \& $\beta = 0$, the equation(\ref{S1}) gives $H_{1}$ surface, while $\alpha = 0$ \& $\beta = 1$, gives corresponding $H_{2}$ surface.

With little manipulation and rescaling, the above equation(\ref{S1}) takes the form
\begin{equation}\label{S}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\eta fy^ {2}+ \frac{1}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)\left(\left( z-\rho\right) ^2 - {\rho}^2\right)
\end{equation}
where, $\eta =  \frac{\beta}{\alpha}$  and  $\rho =\frac{b\eta}{\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}$.
It is clear that $\alpha = 1$ \& $\beta = 0$ $\Rightarrow \eta = 0$. This gives the surface $H_{1}$ of equation(\ref{hh}). While $\alpha = 0$ \& $\beta = 1$ $\Rightarrow \eta = \infty$. This gives the surface $H_{2}$ of equation(\ref{hh}).

All the possible surfaces are not qualitatively different. They fall under only four types of surfaces, namely
\begin{enumerate}
\item {\bf{Cylindrical Surface:}} For $\eta = 0$ with $\frac{c}{f} < 0$, the equation(\ref{S}) reduces to 
\begin{equation}\label{Scy}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2} - \frac{1}{2}\frac{c}{f}z^2 
\end{equation}
 The above equation represents a cylinder with its axis as y-axis, $\frac{c}{f} < 0$.
\item {\bf{Paraboloid:}} For $\eta = \frac{c}{f}$, the equation(\ref{S}) reduces to
\begin{equation}\label{Spa}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2} c y^ {2}+ -\frac{c}{f} b z
\end{equation} 
This equation represents a paraboloid with axis z as its axis.
\item {\bf{Ellipsoid :}} For $\eta > \frac{c}{f}$, the equation(\ref{S}) reduces to
\begin{equation}\label{Sel}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) + \frac{1}{2}\eta{b}\rho =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\eta fy^ {2}+ \frac{1}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)\left( z-\rho\right) ^2
\end{equation}
This is nothing but an equation of ellipsoid with centre at $0,0,\rho$
\item {\bf{Hyperboloid :}} For $\eta = 0$ and $\frac{c}{f}>0$, the equation(\ref{S}) reduces to
\begin{equation}\label{Shy1}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2} - \frac{1}{2}\frac{c}{f}z^2 
\end{equation}
It is a two sheets hyperboloid, while for $\eta < \frac{c}{f}$, the equation (\ref{S}) reduces to the following equation of hyperboloid:
\begin{equation}\label{S}
	S(x,y,z;\eta) =  \frac{1}{2}x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\eta fy^ {2}- \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{c}{f}-\eta\right)\left(\left( z+\rho\right) ^2 - {\rho}^2\right)
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

It is to be noted that if one surface, $H_1 = H_1(0)$ is hyperboloid, then the other $H_2 = H_2(0)$ will necessarily be cylindrical or ellipsoid and vise versa. Therefore we look for intersection of cylindrical / ellipsoid and hyperboloid surfaces to get the trajectories.


 The intersection of any two transformed Nambu surfaces are shown below in FIG.\ref{fig:6}. Here, it is found that the shape of  intersecting orbits in all cases are same which is equivalent to the ND-orbit of the four-wing system.

