Since it is the relative orientation of the two surfaces that what matters, we can consider the motion of one surface with respect to the other. Let, the hyperbolic surface is unchanged for all times $t > 0$. But the cylindric surface that is specified by its radius is changing with t. From a physical point of view, the radius is proportional to the energy of the system. Due to dissipation, the radius changes. A trajectory starts from an arbitrary initial point in the state space, converges into the intersection of the two surfaces. Finally, the trajectory converges into the desired intersection of the two surfaces and is stable. The FIG.\ref{fig:8} illustrate the dynamics of the intersection of the surfaces as time passes. 

%When the system evolution proceeds and the diameter of the cylinder changes in time such that the closed trajectory will be deformed(see FIG. \ref{fig:7}).  In the next time step the shrinking diameter of the cylinder leads to the dividing of the trajectory into two parts. At the end the diameter of the cylinder reaches its minimum and the intersection corresponds to the limit cycle of the Nambu canonucal dissipative system. Each transient trajectory converges to the intersection of the cylinder and the hyperbolic shape (see FIG. \ref{fig:9}). In this paper we present a concept for the construction of systems with limit cycles.From a mathematical point these characteristics can be interpreted as invariants of motion of a dynamical system.

The collection of trajectories at different time intervals due to dissipation can also be put in a more concise mathematical form. This is owing to the fact that dissipation is in a simple linear form in this problem.\\


The dynamical equations(\ref{nambu}) can also be written in the following components form

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x}=\left(\vec\grad{H_{1}}\cross\vec\grad{H_{2}}\right)_{1} -{\eta}_1 x\\
\dot{y}=\left(\vec\grad{H_{1}}\cross\vec\grad{H_{2}}\right)_{2} -{\eta}_2 y\\
\dot{z}=\left(\vec\grad{H_{1}}\cross\vec\grad{H_{2}}\right)_{3} -{\eta}_3 z\\
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where  $\eta_1 = -a$, $\eta_2 = -d$ \& $\eta_3 = -e$ for the present system.\\

We define new variables u, v \& w, called comoving, and new time $\tau$. These are related to the old variables x, y, \& z and time t as : \\

$ u = e^{\eta_1 t}x,\;  v = e^{\eta_2 t}y,\;  w = e^{\eta_3 t}z $, \& $\tau = \frac{1}{\eta_1 + \eta_2+ \eta_3}e^{\left(\eta_1+ \eta_2 + \eta_3\right)}$\; \\

In these new variables, the dynamical system becomes\\
\begin{equation}
\label{eq2}
 \frac{d}{d\tau}(u,v,w)= \vec\grad_{\vec q} {H_{1}}\cross\vec\grad_{\vec q}{H_{2}}
\end{equation}

where $\vec q \equiv u,v,w$ and Hamiltonians are explicit time dependent i.e. $H_{i} = H_{i}\left(e^{-\eta_1 t}u, e^{-\eta_2 t}v, e^{-\eta_3t}w\right)$ , i = 1,2 .

Equation(\ref{eq2}) is equivalent to equation(\ref{NDeq}) and implies that the (u, v, w) phase space volume elements are conserved. 

Only difference between these two is that the later equation, in terms of new variables,, defines non-dissipative trajectories at different instant and so this alone gives the full trajectory as intersection of continuously deforming surfaces.

\subsection{\label{sec4d}Localization of four-wing Attractor}
Since an attractor is a bounded region in phase-space, the coordinates $x$, $y$, $z$ assume values between certain maxima and minima. This implies the surface $S $ can be traced with a radius vector $\vec{R}$, having certain maximum and minimum values. So, the four-wing attractor is also bounded by the volume enclosed by the maximum and minimum positions of these surfaces.

In the absence of dissipation, the surfaces $H_1 = H_{10}$, $H_2 = H_{20}$ and $S = S_0$ are constants, where $H_{10}$, $H_{20}$ and $S_0$ are respective initials values. The rate of change, $\Dot{S} = \grad{S}.\vec{v}$ is then zero for non-dissipative system. This is indeed comes out to be true when $\Dot{S}$ is evaluated using the equations(\ref{NDeq}, \ref{S}).

For the actual dynamical system, including the dissipative part, $\dot{S}$ may not be zero. But if the attractor is localized in a region of the phase space, we expect $\Dot{S}$ to be zero for a set of values of the coordinates $x$, $y$, $z$ ($=X$, $Y$, $Z$ respectively). This in terns gives a critical value of S, $S = S_c$ and $\Dot{S} = 0$ at $X$, $Y$, $Z$. If $S_c$ indicates enclosed localized region, $\dot{S}$ maintains the same sign either for $S>S_c$ or $S<S_c$. If this condition is ensured, we can be sure that the attractor is localised. 

$X$, $Y$, $Z$, and $\lambda$  may be found using the Lagrangian multiplier method with the constraint, $\dot{S} = 0$ at $X$, $Y$, $Z$ while applying the full dynamical equations(\ref{eq1}). Here $\lambda$ is Lagrangian multiplier. Differentiating the equation(\ref{S}) and using the equations(\ref{eq1}), we get

\begin{equation}\label{Sdot}
	\dot{S}\left(x,y,z;\eta\right) =  ax ^{2}+\eta fdy^ {2}+ e\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)\left(\left( z-\frac{1}{2}\rho\right) ^2 - \left(\frac{1}{2}\rho\right)^2\right)
\end{equation}

Setting $\grad{S} + \lambda\grad{\dot{S}} = 0$, we get various solutions for $X$, $Y$, $Z$, and $\lambda$. Once we get these solutions, we can find $S_c$ by putting these solutions in the equation(\ref{S}).

Four simplest values of $X$, $Y$, $Z$, $\lambda$ and $S_c$  are tabulated in the table \ref{T1}.
