We can now show that a surface, given by 
\begin{equation}\label{SSc}
S\left(x,y,z,\eta\right) = S_c = \frac{1}{2}x ^{2}+\frac{1}{2}\eta fy^ {2}+ \frac{1}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)\left(\left( z-\rho\right) ^2 - {\rho}^2\right)
\end{equation}
 is such that $\dot{S}$ has same sign for all values of $x,y,z$ for certain range of system parameters. 

{\bf{Case-I}:} For the case of $S_c = 0$, the equation(\ref{SSc}) takes the form

\begin{equation}\label{SC1}
	x ^{2}+\eta fy^ {2}+ \left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right) \left( z-\rho\right) ^2 = \left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right){\rho}^2
\end{equation}

Using the equations(\ref{Sdot} and \ref{SC1}) we can write
\begin{equation}\label{Sdot1}
	\dot{S}\left(x,y,z;\eta\right) =  \left(a-\frac{e}{2}\right)x ^{2}+\eta f\left(d-\frac{e}{2}\right)y^ {2}+ \frac{e}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2
\end{equation}

The equation(\ref{Sdot1}) shows that $\dot{S}>0$ for all x,y,z only when (i) $\eta >\frac{c}{f}$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $d>\frac{e}{2}$, or (ii) $\eta = \frac{c}{f}$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $d>\frac{e}{2}$, or (iii) $\eta = 0$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{c}{f} < 0$ . For these three conditions the respective localized surfaces is Ellipsoid, Paraboloid or Cylindrical.

{\bf{Case-II}:} For the case of $S_c = -\frac{1}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right){\rho}^2$, the equation(\ref{SSc}) takes the form

\begin{equation}\label{SC2}
	x ^{2}+\eta fy^{2}+ \left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right) \left( z-\rho\right)^2 = 0
\end{equation}

Using the equations(\ref{Sdot} and \ref{SC2}), we can write
\begin{equation}\label{Sdot2}
	\dot{S}\left(x,y,z;\eta\right) =  \left(a-\frac{e}{2}\right)x ^{2}+\eta f\left(d-\frac{e}{2}\right)y^ {2}+ \frac{e}{2}\left(\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2 - \frac{\eta^2b^2}{\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}\right)
\end{equation}

The equation(\ref{Sdot2}) shows that $\dot{S}>0$ for all x,y,z only when $\eta =0$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{c}{f}<0$. In this case then the localized surface is cylindrical.

{\bf{Case-III}:} For the case of $S_c = \frac{\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}{8d\left(e-d\right)}\left(\rho{\left(e-2d\right)}\right)^2$, the equation(\ref{SSc}) takes the form

\begin{equation}\label{SC3}
	\frac{1}{2}\left(x ^{2}+\eta fy^ {2}+ \left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2\right) - {\eta}bz = \frac{\rho\eta{b}}{8d}\frac{\left(e-2d\right)^2}{\left(e-d\right)} 
\end{equation}

Using the equations(\ref{Sdot} and \ref{SC3}) we can write
\begin{equation}\label{Sdot3}
	\dot{S}\left(x,y,z;\eta\right) =  \left(a-\frac{e}{2}\right)x ^{2}+\eta f\left(d-\frac{e}{2}\right)y^ {2}+ \frac{e}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2 + \frac{eb^2{\eta}^2}{8d\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}\frac{\left(e-2d\right)^2}{\left(e-d\right)}
\end{equation}

The equation(\ref{Sdot3}) shows that $\dot{S}>0$ for all x,y,z only when either (i) $\eta =0$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{c}{f}<0$ or (ii) $\eta > \frac{c}{f}$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{e}{2}< d> e$ . In the first case, the localized surface is cylindrical whereas in the second case it is Ellipsoid.

{\bf{Case-IV}:} For the case of $S_c = \frac{\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}{8a\left(e-a\right)}\left(\rho{\left(e-2a\right)}\right)^2$, the equation(\ref{SSc}) takes the form

\begin{equation}\label{SC4}
	\frac{1}{2}\left(x ^{2}+\eta fy^ {2}+ \left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2\right) - {\eta}bz = \frac{\rho\eta{b}}{8a}\frac{\left(e-2a\right)^2}{\left(e-a\right)} 
\end{equation}

Using the equations(\ref{Sdot} and \ref{SC4}) we can write
\begin{equation}\label{Sdot4}
	\dot{S}\left(x,y,z;\eta\right) =  \left(a-\frac{e}{2}\right)x ^{2}+\eta f\left(d-\frac{e}{2}\right)y^ {2}+ \frac{e}{2}\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)z^2 + \frac{eb^2{\eta}^2}{8a\left(\eta-\frac{c}{f}\right)}\frac{\left(e-2a\right)^2}{\left(e-a\right)}
\end{equation}

The equation(\ref{Sdot4}) shows that $\dot{S}>0$ for all x,y,z only when either (i) $\eta =0$, $a>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{c}{f}<0$ or (ii) $\eta > \frac{c}{f}$, $d>\frac{e}{2}$ and $\frac{e}{2}< a> e$ . In the first case, the localized surface is cylindrical whereas in the second case it is Ellipsoid.


\section{\label{sec5}Conclusion}
In this study we consider a complex three dimensional nonlinear dynamical system which produces more complex structure in the phase space than widely studied Lorenz system. Its trajectories lies on four-wing geometrical structure unlike Lorenz system which produces two wings butterfly effect. With the aid of standard numerical techniques, calculations of time series, Lyapunov exponents, Poincaré maps, bifurcation diagrams are carried out. We demonstrated that the chaotic behaviour of the system shows its four-wing complex geometrical shape of the attractor in the phase space for certain range of parameters values. The geometry of the attractor is further studied analytically in details using the theory of Nambu mechanics. Our analytical solutions for Nambu doublets (Hamiltonian kind of functions) do verify the numerical results. We have explicitly demonstrated, how the intersections of these functions generate four-wing structure in the phase space. For this purpose we adopted two steps approach. In the first step, we constructed the two Nambu functions for the non-dissipative parts of the sytem which are constant of motion, and found their intersection as a closed orbit. We showed that these Nambu functions can be transformed by an unitary transformation matrix, giving rise to different surfaces. The transformation is such that their intersection orbit is invariat giving rise to invariant dynamics. Therefore the transformation is canonical. In the second part, we included the dissipative part and showed that the Nambu functions become time dependent, producing full dynamics and the attractor. We have also showed how the attractor is localized in the phase space and found the conditions for the localization. We have explicitly showed that the localized surface can be of only four types of surfaces, namely cylindrical, ellipsoid, paraboloid and hyperboloid. These surfaces are produced due to invariant properties of the Nambu functions and these depend on the transformation matrix. We find that an attractor is confined in the phase space only when the parameters of the system satisfy the conditions $a >\frac{e}{2}$, $\frac{e}{2}< d < e$ and $ \eta \le \frac{c}{f}$ or $ \eta \ge \frac{c}{f}$. $\eta$ is a parameter of the transformation matrix. These analytically obtained values agree with our numerical calculations.


