\subsection{Bias Metrics and Bias measures}

%Bias measures are used to `detect' (or diagnose) and to 'mitigate' (or intervene)~\cite{barocas2019fairness}. 
%When used for detection purposes, bias measures can be applied during model development or post-hoc to test models and applications in order to gain insights into the limits of their performance. When used in this way, bias measures are a critical component of bias evaluations, which again are necessary to detect sources of unfairness and potential discrimination in ML models.
\textit{Bias measures} (also called fairness criteria) that quantify and measure bias are calculated from statistical \textit{base metrics}. Base metrics can be disaggregated (i.e. separated) across speaker groups to evaluate model performance across demographics \cite{verma2018fairness}. Base metrics in speech recognition are mostly error rates that also measure system performance. Bias measures then differences or ratios between the base metrics of a group and a reference group, or overall performance to compare model performance across groups.
In ASR, the primary base metric is WER, with modifications, such as excluding deletion errors~\cite{Tatman2017EffectsCaptions}. Studies also utilize PER \cite{YuChan2022TrainingEnglishes}, CER \cite{Gao2022SeamlessRecognition}, and perplexity to evaluate language models~\cite{koenecke2020racial}. Additionally, an average model confidence score is employed to estimate the probability of correct output without relying solely on ground truth transcriptions~\cite{Dheram2022TowardDisparities}. 

\noindent\textit{Group-based measures and Meta-measures:}
Bias measures can be group-based, meaning that  they are employed to measure bias for individuals~\cite{Mehrabi2019Survey}, under the fairness assumption that similar individuals should receive similar predictions. There are several constraints to take into account when using group-based bias measures. Most measures in the literature have been developed for binary decision outcomes and binary groups (i.e. one group with a protected attribute and an unprotected group) which can make model evaluation and selection extremely difficult if bias must be evaluated for many models, across many groups \cite{Lum2022}. This challenge is resolved by \textit{meta-measures} that aggregate bias measures across all groups into a single measure to evaluate overall model bias. However, when group sizes are not equal, meta-measures can be biased themselves \cite{Lum2022}.

