\subsection{Decay properties of $^{257}$Db}

Earlier studies~\cite{hessberger2001decay,gates2008comparison,streicher2006synthesis} established that the $\alpha$-decay spectrum of $^{257}$Db consists of two distinct groups, characterised by different decay times (see Fig.\ref{Tlog}) and energies (see Fig.\ref{Ea_fit}). The first is a low-energy, double-humped structure spanning 8860--9100~keV, with a half-life of 1.8~s, while the second is a sharp high-energy peak at 9150~keV, with a shorter half-life of 653~ms. The former is associated with the decay of a high-spin state, which undergoes favoured $\alpha$ decays down to $^{245}$Es, whereas the latter is attributed to the decay of the 1/2$^-$[521] Nilsson state, as shown in Fig.~\ref{Level}.

