\subsection{Overview}
Given a set of LDR images of a dynamic scene $\left\{L_1, L_2, \ldots, L_n\right\}$ with different exposures, HDR imaging aims to generate an HDR image $H$ aligned to a prescribed reference image. Fig.~\ref{Fig2} shows an overview of the proposed EAFNet. 
Our proposed EAFNet consists of three main subnetworks: the pre-alignment subnetwork, the asymmetric cross-feature fusion subnetwork, and the restoration subnetwork.
%In the pre-alignment network, we focus on exploring exposure information and extracting meaningful details from the non-reference image, aiming to improve detail preservation and color accuracy. Global luminance alignment (GLA) is proposed to introduce consistent brightness features across varying exposures, while an exposure-guide feature selection module (EFSM) explores the correlation between inputs and exposure information to focus on the most relevant regions for HDR fusion. 
